# One module of the project leaves a meta unsolved, and another imports
# it.  Agda writes no interface for a module like that and will not
# reuse the check, so the importer has it checked a second time -- in
# the one state the builder checks the whole project in, which used to
# be an `Ambiguous name` internal error that took the whole build down
# and left the project with no index at all.
#
# What it should do is what it does for every other module that will not
# load: index what it could read, and record the rest.  Every query is
# scoped to `Lib`, so that the counts do not depend on how many modules
# Agda's own primitive library happens to have.
!file Lib/Main.agda
{"method":"load","params":{"file":"%FILE%"}}
# The build gets through the whole project: Lib.Main and Lib.Unsolved
# are rendered, and Lib.Uses -- which cannot be loaded, since importing
# Lib.Unsolved raises its unsolved metas -- is recorded, not indexed.
# Only Lib.Main has an interface, so it alone is `indexed`; the other
# two are `unindexed`, which is the honest answer for a module Agda
# would have to check again.
{"method":"buildIndex","params":{"module":"Lib"}}
# The point of the whole exercise: a name defined in the module with the
# unsolved meta is still there to be found.
{"method":"findNames","params":{"pattern":"double","module":"Lib"}}
# And so is one from the file that was loaded.
{"method":"findNames","params":{"pattern":"identity","module":"Lib"}}
{"method":"close"}
